<!-- Section 1: Abstract -->
<h2 id="abstract">1. Abstract</h2>
<p>This report presents the results of a comprehensive multiphysics simulation campaign
studying waveform-controlled PEDOT electropolymerization in a two-compartment salt-bridge
bipolar electrochemical system. A staged physics model—progressing from pure
diffusion-reaction (Stage A) through electric field coupling (Stage B), Nernst-Planck
migration (Stage C), and Butler-Volmer electrode kinetics (Stage D)—was employed to
quantify how waveform frequency, electrolyte diffusion coefficient, and cell geometry
influence the morphological outcomes of PEDOT film growth.</p>
<p>The dependent variables—Anisotropy Index (AI), Uniformity Index (UI), depletion zone depth,
and timescale ratio—were systematically measured across a full factorial experimental design
comprising {{ n_runs }} simulation runs. Statistical analysis via three-way ANOVA and
multiple regression identifies the dominant factors and their interactions, while Pareto
front optimization highlights conditions balancing uniformity with growth rate.</p>

<!-- Section 2: Glossary -->
<h2 id="glossary">2. Glossary of Variables</h2>
<dl class="glossary">
<dt>\(C\) [mol/m&sup3;]</dt><dd>EDOT monomer concentration</dd>
<dt>\(D\) [m&sup2;/s]</dt><dd>Diffusion coefficient</dd>
<dt>\(V\) [V]</dt><dd>Electric potential</dd>
<dt>\(\mathbf{E}\) [V/m]</dt><dd>Electric field vector</dd>
<dt>\(j\) [A/m&sup2;]</dt><dd>Current density at electrode</dd>
<dt>\(\eta\) [V]</dt><dd>Overpotential</dd>
<dt>\(h\) [m]</dt><dd>Film thickness</dd>
<dt>\(R\) [mol/(m&sup3;&middot;s)]</dt><dd>Reaction rate</dd>
<dt>\(f\) [Hz]</dt><dd>Waveform frequency</dd>
<dt>\(V_{DC}\) [V]</dt><dd>DC offset potential</dd>
<dt>\(V_{AC}\) [V]</dt><dd>AC amplitude</dd>
<dt>AI [-]</dt><dd>Anisotropy Index = \(h_{max}/h_{avg}\)</dd>
<dt>UI [m]</dt><dd>Uniformity Index = std(\(h\))</dd>
<dt>\(\tau_{diff}\) [s]</dt><dd>Diffusion timescale = \(L^2/D\)</dd>
<dt>\(\tau_{rxn}\) [s]</dt><dd>Reaction timescale = \(L/k\)</dd>
</dl>

<!-- Section 3: Research Question -->
<h2 id="rq">3. Research Question &amp; Hypothesis</h2>
<p><strong>Research Question:</strong> How do variations in waveform parameters, including frequency,
amplitude, and DC offset, influence the field-diffusion dynamics such as electrolyte
composition and ion diffusion in a modelled two-compartment salt-bridge electropolymerization
system and predict tendencies toward uniform or anisotropic PEDOT growth for application in
wearable biosensors?</p>
<p><strong>Hypothesis:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Uniformity</strong> (low AI, low UI): Achieved when \(\tau_{diff} \ll \tau_{rxn}\), corresponding
to high AC frequencies and low viscosity electrolytes, ensuring rapid monomer replenishment
at the electrode interface.</li>
<li><strong>Anisotropy</strong> (high AI): Maximum in mass-transport-limited systems where
\(\tau_{diff} > \tau_{rxn}\), characterized by low-frequency AC signals with high DC values,
leading to tip-induced ion depletion and dendritic growth.</li>
</ul>

<!-- Section 4: Governing Equations -->
<h2 id="equations">4. Governing Equations</h2>

<h3>4.1 Stage A: Diffusion-Reaction</h3>
<div class="equation">
\[ \frac{\partial C}{\partial t} = D \nabla^2 C - R \]
</div>
<p>where \(R = k_{rxn} \cdot C\) at the electrode boundary, zero elsewhere.</p>

<h3>4.2 Stage B: Electric Field (Laplace)</h3>
<div class="equation">
\[ \nabla^2 V = 0 \]
</div>
<div class="equation">
\[ \mathbf{E} = -\nabla V \]
</div>
<p>Field-enhanced reaction: \( R = k_{rxn} \cdot C \cdot (1 + \beta |\mathbf{E}|) \)</p>

<h3>4.3 Stage C: Nernst-Planck Migration</h3>
<div class="equation">
\[ \frac{\partial C}{\partial t} = \nabla \cdot \left( D \nabla C + \frac{zFD}{RT} C \nabla V \right) - R \]
</div>

<h3>4.4 Stage D: Butler-Volmer Electrode Kinetics</h3>
<div class="equation">
\[ j = j_0 \left(\frac{C}{C_{ref}}\right) \left[ \exp\left(\frac{\alpha n F \eta}{RT}\right) - \exp\left(-\frac{(1-\alpha)nF\eta}{RT}\right) \right] \]
</div>
<p>Film growth rate:</p>
<div class="equation">
\[ \frac{dh}{dt} = \frac{|j| \cdot M_{EDOT}}{n \cdot F \cdot \rho_{PEDOT}} \]
</div>

<!-- Section 5: Geometry -->
<h2 id="geometry">5. Geometry &amp; Boundary Conditions</h2>
<pre style="font-family:monospace;background:#f5f5f5;padding:15px;border-radius:5px;font-size:0.85em;">
  +---LEFT COMPT.---+     +--BRIDGE--+     +--RIGHT COMPT.--+
  |                 |     |          |     |                 |
  | C = C_bulk (BC) |=====| BPE HERE |=====| C = C_bulk (BC) |
  | V = +V(t)/2     |     | (float)  |     | V = -V(t)/2     |
  |                 |     |          |     |                 |
  +-----------------+     +----------+     +-----------------+
  x=0               x=cw  x=cw+bl    x=2cw+bl
</pre>
<p>Insulating walls: \(\partial V/\partial n = 0\), \(\partial C/\partial n = 0\).</p>

<!-- Section 6: Waveforms -->
<h2 id="waveforms">6. Waveforms &amp; Parameter Matrix</h2>
<div class="equation">
\[ V(t) = V_{DC} + V_{AC} \sin(2\pi f t) \]
</div>

<!-- Section 7: Numerical Methods -->
<h2 id="numerical">7. Numerical Methods</h2>
<p>The simulation uses finite-difference discretization on a uniform 2D structured grid.
The Laplace equation is solved via Successive Over-Relaxation (SOR). Mass transport uses
a semi-implicit scheme with Crank-Nicolson (θ=0.5) for diffusion and explicit operator
splitting for the migration term. Time integration stability is monitored via the Fourier
number \(Fo = D \Delta t / \Delta x^2\).</p>

